    "topic_code": "2.1",
    "topic_title": "Types of Chemical Bonds",
    "stem": "Hydrogen and chlorine have electronegativities of 2.2 and 3.2, respectively. The atoms share a pair of electrons in an H–Cl bond. Which description best accounts for the bond’s electron distribution and bonding character?",
    "choices": [
     {
      "choice_key": "A",
      "choice_text": "The bond is polar covalent, with a partial negative charge on H and some ionic character.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Reversed partial charges: the more electronegative atom attracts the shared electrons more strongly, so Cl, not H, carries the partial negative charge. Fix: Compare electronegativities and assign the partial negative charge to the higher value."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "B",
      "choice_text": "The bond is nonpolar covalent because both atoms are nonmetals.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Nonmetal shortcut: two nonmetals generally form a covalent bond, but that does not establish equal electron sharing. Their unequal electronegativities make this bond polar. Fix: Compare the atoms’ electronegativities before deciding whether a covalent bond is polar."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "C",
      "choice_text": "The bond is polar covalent, with a partial negative charge on Cl and some ionic character.",
      "is_correct": true,
      "rationale": "Cl is more electronegative than H, so it attracts the shared electrons more strongly and carries a partial negative charge. This unequal sharing makes the bond polar covalent; the resulting charge separation gives it some ionic character."
     },
     {
      "choice_key": "D",
      "choice_text": "The bond is polar covalent, with a partial negative charge on Cl but no ionic character.",
      "is_correct": false,
      "rationale": "Separate-box misconception: covalent and ionic bonding are not completely separate categories. Unequal sharing produces partial charges, so a polar covalent bond has some ionic character without complete electron transfer. Fix: Check whether unequal sharing creates charge separation before claiming a bond has no ionic character."
     }
    ]
   },
